{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": [
      "node"
    ],
    "allowJs": true,
    // Tests importing `scripts/test-support/git-sandbox.ts` by relative path need the literal `.ts`
    // extension (there is no `dist` to resolve to -- it is a repo-root helper, never built, per the
    // comment on that include entry below). Safe only because this program is `noEmit: true` and never
    // builds a package: each package's own tsconfig excludes its tests, so this flag cannot reach a
    // package's published `dist`.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  },
  // `packages/*/src/**/*.ts` is here because each package's own tsconfig EXCLUDES its tests (they must not
  // reach a consumer's node_modules), and the root program only had "src" — so as packages were extracted in
  // M2-M5, about 25 test files silently stopped being type-checked and `npm run typecheck` reported clean
  // over them. Measured with `tsc --listFiles`: 0 package tests in the program.
  //
  // Deliberately `**/*.ts` and not the directory: `allowJs` is on, so naming the directory would newly
  // type-check 14 worker `.mjs` files as JS, which is a separate change with its own fallout.
  // `src` is gone as of M8 — every source file now lives in a package.
  "include": [
    "packages/*/src/**/*.ts",
    // #1135: the nightly-only test population lives beside `src/`, never inside it, because the PR suite's
    // glob (`packages/*/src/**/*.test.ts`) has no exclusion syntax and a directory outside it is the only
    // shape the resolver cannot pick up. Typechecked all the same.
    "packages/*/nightly/**/*.ts",
    // THE GATE SCRIPTS, which were outside the typecheck entirely — so `tsc --noEmit` passed on
    // `audit-rule-coverage.ts` while it called a function whose import did not exist, and only
    // running it found out. `score-rules.ts`, `audit-rule-coverage.ts` and
    // `check-real-page-findings.ts` ARE the rules gates, so "the gate is not checked" is this
    // repo's most-named defect applied to the thing doing the checking.
    "packages/*/scripts/**/*.ts",
    // AND THE .mjs, or `// @ts-check` is a comment.
    "packages/*/src/**/*.mjs",
    "packages/*/scripts/**/*.mjs",
    "packages/*/bin/**/*.mjs",
    "packages/*/*.mjs",
    // #1149: `nightly/` got a `.ts` entry above when #1135 created it and a `.mjs` entry never,
    // because the directory held only tests. The first non-test file to land there — the tenant
    // manifest — fell outside the program, so its `// @ts-check` would have been a comment and
    // `tsc --noEmit` would have reported clean over it. `typecheck-coverage.test.ts` caught it; the
    // entry is here rather than the file moved, because every other `.mjs` population has one.
    "packages/*/nightly/**/*.mjs",
    // The shared git-sandbox test helper lives at the repo root, not inside a package, because it is
    // imported by tests across four packages (lab, judge, control, worker-fleet) and none of those may
    // depend on each other -- see ADR 0004. Same shape as the gate scripts above: a helper eleven tests
    // rely on to avoid corrupting the real repo is not one to leave outside the typecheck.
    "scripts/test-support/**/*.ts",
    // #189: top-level `scripts/` was the third population-boundary gap in two days (#164, #174), and the
    // worst of the three -- 22 CLIs, several ALREADY carrying `@type` JSDoc annotations that nothing
    // enforced (`row-claim.mjs`'s injected `run` seam, `merge-guard.mjs`'s whole pure verdict). Being in
    // this list alone changes nothing without `checkJs` or a per-file `// @ts-check` pragma -- see the
    // comment on the `.mjs` entries above ("or `// @ts-check` is a comment"). This is that half: every
    // file gets the pragma, is fixed, or carries `// @ts-nocheck` with a stated reason.
    "scripts/**/*.mjs"
  ]
}
